      "created_timestamp": 1758652316566,
      "description": "Homeostatic interactions between injured kidney epithelium with its surrounding milieu is an\nimportant determinant of repair outcome. Pathogenic or unbalanced signaling may promote\nunresolved states of inflammation and fibrosis, leading to chronic disease. To better understand\nresolved versus unresolved repair states in the human kidney, we have used multiple single cell\nand spatial modalities to interrogate more than 350 samples from more than 250 participants\n(>1.5 million cells). We further integrate these with multiomic profiles for mouse acute to chronic\nkidney injury and aging (>300k cells) and spatial transcriptomic profiles of 150 human biopsies.\nThe resultant cross-species atlas provides functional pathways for segments along the nephron,\nas well as gene regulatory networks and chromatin states for tubules, fibroblasts and immune\ncells as they transition from injury to recovery or failed / unresolved states. Several states were\nfound associated with distinct pathological features, showed dynamic distributions between\nacute kidney injury (AKI) and chronic kidney disease (CKD) and were organized into distinct\nspatial niches. Gene regulatory analyses prioritized transcription factor activities and target\ngenes establishing these states, including the pioneer factors SOX4, SOX9 and KLFs. These\ngene regulatory processes were further linked to clinical outcomes, identifying recovery features\nin diabetic patients undergoing treatment with SGLT2 inhibitors and secreted markers predictive\nof AKI and CKD progression from plasma proteomics.",
